An unspecified address (`http://0.0.0.0:3000`, which many dev servers print as their listen URL, or `http://[::]:3000`) is now probed as given. On Windows, `connect()` to `INADDR_ANY`/`in6addr_any` fails with `WSAEADDRNOTAVAIL`, so a running server shows as dead. Before this change it probed `127.0.0.1` and worked. Treating an unspecified literal like a hostname fixes that:

```suggestion
    if let Ok(ip) = host.parse::<IpAddr>()
        && !ip.is_unspecified()
    {
```

Covers both socket-domain branches of `probe_addr`. Nothing else in the suite reaches it, because of the env gate:

```suggestion
    }

    #[test]
    fn probe_addr_detects_listening_loopback_sockets() {
        let v4 = std::net::TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:0").unwrap();
        assert!(probe_addr(v4.local_addr().unwrap()));
        let v6 = std::net::TcpListener::bind("[::1]:0").unwrap();
        assert!(probe_addr(v6.local_addr().unwrap()));
    }
```
